-- js-effects.rofl — THE FIFTH LAYER, AND THE FIRST ONE THAT IS AN ORDER.
--
-- `layer(effect).` is the whole declaration, and it opens one hundred cells:
-- one per declared node kind, because the shape axis does not apply here (the
-- argument is in the LOCAL AXIS section below, and it is a refusal with a date
-- on it rather than an omission).
--
-- WHOSE DECLARATION THIS IS, and it is recorded because the provenance did not
-- arrive with the line. `layer(L)` is the owner's alone — a standing constraint
-- in HANDOFF.md — and the owner declared `layer(effect)` on 2026-09-09. The
-- line did not reach `origin/modeljs`: it was written in the owner's working
-- tree AFTER the push and reverted there so that nothing competed with this
-- branch, and the instruction that came with the correction was that the
-- declaration and the ownership of its hundred cells belong in ONE commit,
-- because a layer with unowned cells is not a valid state of this queue.
-- Measured by the owner before reverting: `open_cell[audit]` 6 -> 100.
--
-- It is declared HERE and not in facts/js-statements.rofl, where the owner's
-- own copy sat, for one reason that is not taste: `layer(effect)` and the
-- hundred verdicts under it must be in the SAME pack, or a world that loads the
-- declaration without the verdicts reports a hundred cells nobody owns. That is
-- exactly the state the owner's correction says is invalid, and it is the shape
-- `layer(controlflow)` and `layer(modules)` already have — each in the fact
-- pack of its own layer.
layer(effect).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE TAXONOMY IS KOKA'S AND IT IS NOT OURS TO INVENT
--
-- Settled by `f_the_effect_taxonomy_already_exists_and_it_is_a_lattice` on
-- 2026-09-05 and not re-litigated here. The set is closed, named and ORDERED:
-- `total`, `exn`, `div`, `ndet`, `alloc<h>`, `read<h>`, `write<h>`, `io`, with
-- the aliases `pure = <div, exn>`, `st<h> = <rd, wr, heap<h>>` and
-- `io = <st<global>, div, exn, ndet>`.
--
-- A LATTICE WITH A BOTTOM AND A TOP IS THE POINT. Every question this layer is
-- asked — what is a body's effect given its statements', is this call at least
-- as effectful as that one — is a JOIN or a COMPARISON, and a flat list of
-- labels gives neither. So what is modelled below is the ORDER, and the labels
-- are what the order is over.
--
-- THE CARRIER IS A ROW, NOT A LABEL. An effect is a SET of labels; the order is
-- set inclusion; the join is union; the bottom is the empty row and the top is
-- the full one. The named points below are LANDMARKS in that lattice, and the
-- family they form is closed under intersection and contains the full row —
-- which is exactly the condition (a Moore family) that makes the least NAME
-- above a union always exist and be unique. That is not asserted here as a
-- claim about the design: `join_missing[audit]`, `join_ambiguous[audit]`,
-- `meet_missing[audit]` and `meet_ambiguous[audit]` in rules/js-effects.rofl
-- are the four ways it could fail, and they are the acceptance.

edb(eff_heap).
edb(eff_label).
edb(eff_name).
edb(eff_row).
edb(eff_alias).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE HEAP PARAMETER, AND WHERE IT IS REFUSED.
--
-- Koka parameterises three of the eight by a heap: `alloc<h>`, `read<h>`,
-- `write<h>`, and `io` is defined over the GLOBAL one. The parameter is what
-- makes `io`'s definition say anything at all, so it is kept — with two
-- constants and a rule for deciding between them that this model can actually
-- run:
--
--   local   the object is one this program allocates. `may_be_node[flow]`
--           reaches an allocation site for the receiver.
--   global  it does not. An ambient name, an import, a receiver the value layer
--           cannot trace. This is precisely the residue the runtime and globals
--           surfaces are being built to name, which is why the parameter earns
--           its place rather than decorating the labels.
--
-- `alloc` IS NOT PARAMETERISED AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE DEVIATION FROM KOKA.
-- Deciding which heap an allocation lands in is escape analysis, which this
-- model does not have and will not fake; a column whose two values the rules
-- cannot tell apart is the 48-cells-no-questions arithmetic that
-- `axis_applies` exists to prevent, arriving in a label instead of an axis. So
-- `st<h>` here is `<read<h>, write<h>, alloc>` — the deviation is one line and
-- it is this one.
eff_heap(none).
eff_heap(local).
eff_heap(global).

eff_label(exn,   none).
eff_label(div,   none).
eff_label(ndet,  none).
eff_label(alloc, none).
eff_label(read,  local).
eff_label(read,  global).
eff_label(write, local).
eff_label(write, global).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE NAMED LANDMARKS. Fourteen, and eight of them are the singleton row of a
-- label; the other six are DEFINED by `eff_alias` below rather than typed out,
-- so that `pure = <div, exn>` is a definition the model closes rather than a
-- pair of rows a reader has to trust.
eff_name(total).
eff_name(exn).
eff_name(div).
eff_name(ndet).
eff_name(alloc).
eff_name(rd_local).
eff_name(wr_local).
eff_name(rd_global).
eff_name(wr_global).
eff_name(pure).
eff_name(st_local).
eff_name(st_global).
eff_name(io).
eff_name(top).

-- `total` gets no row: it IS the empty one, and that is what makes it the
-- bottom rather than a name for nothing. `eff_name_empty[audit]` guards the
-- difference — any OTHER name that ends up with no row is a misspelt alias
-- silently becoming the bottom, which would be a lie in the safe direction.
eff_row(exn,       exn,   none).
eff_row(div,       div,   none).
eff_row(ndet,      ndet,  none).
eff_row(alloc,     alloc, none).
eff_row(rd_local,  read,  local).
eff_row(wr_local,  write, local).
eff_row(rd_global, read,  global).
eff_row(wr_global, write, global).

-- KOKA'S ALIASES, AS DEFINITIONS. rules/js-effects.rofl closes `eff_row` over
-- this table, so `join(div, exn) = pure` is DERIVED from the definition and not
-- from a third place where the same thing is written down again.
eff_alias(pure, div).
eff_alias(pure, exn).
eff_alias(st_local,  rd_local).
eff_alias(st_local,  wr_local).
eff_alias(st_local,  alloc).
eff_alias(st_global, rd_global).
eff_alias(st_global, wr_global).
eff_alias(st_global, alloc).
eff_alias(io, st_global).
eff_alias(io, div).
eff_alias(io, exn).
eff_alias(io, ndet).
-- ...AND THE TOP, WHICH KOKA DOES NOT NAME AND THIS MODEL NEEDS. `io` is the
-- top of the GLOBAL tower — `total <= pure <= io` — and local state is not
-- under it, which is not an accident of the encoding: Koka's `local<s>` is
-- ENCAPSULATED, discharged by `run`, and that is why `io` does not contain it.
-- A lattice needs a top, so the full row is named; `meet(io, st_local) = alloc`
-- is the derived statement that the two branches share exactly the allocation.
eff_alias(top, io).
eff_alias(top, st_local).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE CONCRETE COLUMN — `http:get` RATHER THAN `io`, AND WHY IT IS A SECOND
-- THING AND NOT A REPLACEMENT.
--
-- The owner's amendment, 2026-09-09: "the Koka lattice is worth refining in a
-- more human-readable language, because `http:get` is somewhat clearer than
-- `io`". Both are kept, because they answer different questions and the model
-- needs both:
--
--   `io`       answers the ALGEBRAIC question — the join of a body's
--              statements, the comparison between two calls. It needs an
--              order. It has one.
--   `http:get` answers the HUMAN question — what does this function actually
--              DO — which `io` answers uselessly. `http:get`, `fs:read` and
--              `console:write` form NO order at all: there is no answer to
--              which of `http:get` and `fs:write` is greater.
--
-- A CONCRETE EFFECT IS A PAIR, NOT AN ATOM. It is (Surface, Operation), and
-- `http:get` is how the pair is WRITTEN for a reader. Manufacturing a joined
-- atom would be making a term out of nothing, which this repository has refused
-- before; the colon is a rendering, so `runtime/report.ts` can print it and no
-- rule has to parse it back.
--
-- AND IT MAPS INTO THE LATTICE. `⊑` is `ambient_effect(Surface, Operation,
-- EffectName)` — one row per member, naming a landmark above. Join and compare
-- in the lattice; show the human the pair.
--
-- THE REAL RISK IS THE OPEN VOCABULARY AND THE CURE IS THE FINDING'S OWN.
-- Koka's set is closed at eight; `http:get` opens an infinite one, which is
-- what `f_the_effect_taxonomy_already_exists_and_it_is_a_lattice` refused when
-- it said the taxonomy is not ours to invent. The same finding names the cure
-- by example — it rejected `UNKNOWN` as a MEMBER because `unknown_type(R,
-- irreducible|ours)` CLASSIFIES an unknown instead of making unknown a kind.
-- So: A CONCRETE EFFECT IS DERIVED, NEVER AUTHORED, and this file authors not
-- one of them.
--
-- WHERE EACH HALF OF THE NAME COMES FROM, per half, because the two halves have
-- different provenance and only one of them is free:
--
--   THE SURFACE HALF IS A FACT and needs no judgement:
--     * a builtin prototype        `prototype_of[flow]` from the receiver's
--                                  KIND, plus `builtin_prototype` — derived in
--                                  the tree TODAY: `array`, `string`, `regexp`,
--                                  `bigint` over ten call sites
--     * an ES intrinsic            the global's own name as `lib.es*.d.ts`
--                                  declares it — `Promise`, `Map`, `JSON`,
--                                  `Math`, `Symbol`, `globalThis`
--     * a host runtime module      the import specifier, or the ambient
--                                  global's name, as `lib.dom.d.ts` and
--                                  `@types/node` declare it — `console`,
--                                  `fetch`, `setTimeout`, `fs`, `path`,
--                                  `process`
--   THE OPERATION HALF is the member name, also a fact.
--   THE MAP INTO THE LATTICE IS NEITHER. Nothing in a `.d.ts` signature says
--   `readFileSync` reads and `writeFileSync` writes; the declaration FILE says
--   which surface a member belongs to and says nothing about its effect. So
--   `ambient_effect` is what the surface packs must supply, member by member,
--   and until they do EVERY ambient call is an open cell with an owner rather
--   than a label. That is the honest answer to the majority case and it is the
--   answer this file gives.
--
-- WHAT THIS OWES grafema's effects-db, the owner's own prior art, which the
-- founding finding already weighed: PURE / MUTATION / IO / THROW / ASYNC /
-- NONDETERMINISTIC / SPAWN / MESSAGE_PASSING / UNKNOWN, split by runtime.
--   * AGREED, and taken: PURE is `total`, MUTATION is `write<h>`, IO is `io`,
--     THROW is `exn`, NONDETERMINISTIC is `ndet`.
--   * AGREED AND PROMOTED: the RUNTIME SPLIT is half of what the amendment
--     asks for, and it is exactly the SURFACE half of a concrete effect. A
--     runtime is not an effect; it is where an effect's name comes from.
--   * AGREED AND MOVED DOWN A LEVEL: SPAWN and MESSAGE_PASSING are not
--     lattice elements, they are CONCRETE effects — `child_process:spawn`,
--     `worker:postMessage` — each mapping into `io`. That is the whole
--     argument for having two columns: they are the names a human wants and
--     the join has nothing to do with them.
--   * REFUSED: ASYNC. The founding finding measured the point — `await` is
--     transparent to value flow and `yield` is not — so async-as-an-effect
--     asserts a uniformity this repository's own measurement contradicts, and
--     a concrete column must not smuggle it back as `async:*`.
--     `concrete_async_smuggled[audit]` in rules/js-effects.rofl is that
--     sentence as a row.
--   * REFUSED: UNKNOWN, on the grounds the finding already gave.
--
-- WHAT THE SURFACE PACKS MUST SUPPLY, stated as the shape this layer reads. The
-- relations are declared `edb` here so that a world holding this pack and no
-- surface reads them as EMPTY rather than as a misspelling — the same reason
-- `unknown_type` and `ast_node` carry one in rules/js-model.rofl.
--
--   surface_origin(Surface, Origin)
--       one row per surface, saying which declaration file it came from.
--       Origin is one of the three `eff_origin` values below.
--   ambient_effect(Surface, Member, EffectName)
--       the `⊑` map: one row per MEMBER, naming the lattice landmark the
--       member's effect is at or below. EffectName must be an `eff_name` —
--       `concrete_unnamed[audit]` says so.
--
--       THE MEMBER IS A STRING AND THE OTHER TWO ARE ATOMS, and this is not a
--       style note: `ambient_effect(fs, readFileSync, io)` LOADS, holds, joins
--       nothing and reports nothing, because the operation half comes from
--       `selects[flow]`, which carries a key as a STRING. The right row is
--       `ambient_effect(fs, "readFileSync", io).` Measured the hard way — the
--       first planted control in test/js-effects.test.ts was written unquoted
--       and derived zero — and it is the defect
--       `f_unpopulatable_sees_the_relation_and_is_blind_to_the_constant`
--       already records: a misspelt CONSTANT in the right lexical form is a
--       well-formed question about a real relation, and nothing objects.
--   ambient_binding(File, Name, Surface)
--       which free NAME in which file is which surface. Needed for
--       `console.log(...)`: the receiver `console` is a free identifier and
--       nothing in this program says what it is. Without this relation the
--       `io` half of this layer cannot be derived at all, which is why
--       `call_expression` and `identifier` are open cells below rather than
--       stamped rows.
edb(surface_origin).
edb(ambient_effect).
edb(ambient_binding).
edb(eff_origin).
-- `lib.es*.d.ts` — an ECMAScript intrinsic. Present in every host.
eff_origin(es_intrinsic).
-- `lib.dom.d.ts` or `@types/node` — a host surface. Present only in its host,
-- which is the runtime split grafema's effects-db already makes.
eff_origin(host_runtime).
-- derived in this tree today, with no `.d.ts` at all: the receiver's KIND fixes
-- the prototype, which is what `w_prototype_of_a_value` closed.
eff_origin(builtin_prototype).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE LOCAL AXIS, REFUSED WITH A DATE ON IT.
--
-- `axis_applies(shape, effect).` IS NOT DECLARED, and the reason is the one the
-- machinery exists to enforce rather than an oversight. A refinement column
-- must EARN its layer: `unearned_axis[audit]` reports an axis that splits a
-- layer into rows all carrying the same answer, and `axis_earns[audit]` needs
-- two shapes of ONE KIND to differ in verdict or in typed reason.
--
-- MEASURED TODAY: the concrete column derives TEN call sites — `array:join`
-- twice, `string:concat`, `string:replaceAll`, `string:substr`,
-- `string:trimLeft`, `regexp:test` twice, `array:at`, `bigint:toString` — and
-- `ambient_effect` is EMPTY, so all ten would carry `not_yet` for the same
-- reason. Ten shapes, one verdict: the column would multiply the table and
-- answer nothing, which is the 48-cells-no-questions arithmetic by name.
--
-- AND THE CONDITION FOR DECLARING IT IS EXACT, which is why this is a refusal
-- and not a shrug: `axis_applies(shape, effect).` becomes correct on the day
-- the surface packs attribute AT LEAST ONE member and leave at least one
-- unattributed — one `handled` shape beside one `not_yet` shape is what
-- `axis_earns` asks for. test/js-effects.test.ts plants exactly that as a
-- positive control, so the sentence above is a runnable claim rather than a
-- prediction.
--
-- The refusal is the same one facts/js-shapes.rofl makes for `dataflow` and it
-- is made here for the same reason, with the corpus measured first rather than
-- the row tried first.

-- ===========================================================================
-- WHAT THE LAYER ANSWERS — 33 CELLS, BY MECHANISM
--
-- rules/js-effects.rofl carries the mechanisms and the audits that keep the
-- vocabulary closed. The split below is by MECHANISM and not by kind, which is
-- the shape facts/js-controlflow.rofl already uses.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- EXN, and it is the one label with an independent oracle. `may_throw[code]`
-- was built by `w_exn_propagation` for the exception question alone; this layer
-- re-derives it as ONE PROJECTION of a general rule — seed, then close over the
-- call graph, minus what a handler discharges. The two must agree ROW FOR ROW,
-- and `eff_exn_only[audit]` / `may_throw_only[audit]` are the two directions.
-- That is a cross-check rather than a restatement: different relations,
-- different rules, and a wrong discharge condition separates them at once.
handled(js, throw_statement,            effect, r_effect_throw).

-- ...AND A HANDLER REMOVES A LABEL FROM A ROW, which is the piece of an effect
-- system that a flat list of labels cannot express at all. Koka types `catch`
-- as `(() -> <exn|e> a, ...) -> e a`: the handler DISCHARGES `exn` and leaves
-- the rest of the row alone. `eff_discharges(catch_clause, exn)` is that
-- sentence, and the propagation rule reads it per LABEL — so a `try` around a
-- call does not swallow the callee's writes, which a single `not caught_here`
-- guard would have done.
handled(js, try_statement,              effect, r_effect_handler).
handled(js, catch_clause,               effect, r_effect_handler).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- DIV. Every loop MAY not terminate, and the model has no termination
-- argument for any of them, so all five seed `div`. This is a MAY-set, which
-- is the vocabulary the whole model uses, and it is stated rather than hidden:
-- `for (let i = 0; i < 2; i += 1)` terminates and this layer says it may not.
-- The over-approximation is in the safe direction for every question `div` is
-- asked, and narrowing it is a termination analysis rather than a rule.
handled(js, while_statement,            effect, r_effect_loop).
handled(js, do_while_statement,         effect, r_effect_loop).
handled(js, for_statement,              effect, r_effect_loop).
handled(js, for_in_statement,           effect, r_effect_loop).
handled(js, for_of_statement,           effect, r_effect_loop).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ALLOC. A fresh MUTABLE IDENTITY is an allocation; a fresh immutable value is
-- not. That distinction is the whole content of these three rows and it is
-- where a mechanical sweep would have gone wrong: `reg_exp_literal` LOOKS like
-- the five literals waived below and is not one of them, because `/x/g`
-- evaluates to a new object with a mutable `lastIndex` every time it is
-- reached. `template_literal` looks like it allocates and does not: a string
-- has no identity to allocate.
handled(js, object_expression,          effect, r_effect_alloc).
handled(js, array_expression,           effect, r_effect_alloc).
handled(js, reg_exp_literal,            effect, r_effect_alloc).

-- ...and the three that allocate a CLOSURE. Evaluating them allocates; their
-- BODY's effect is latent and joins only where the closure is called, which is
-- the distinction `r_effect_latent` makes below and the reason these are not
-- filed with the three above.
handled(js, function_expression,        effect, r_effect_closure).
handled(js, arrow_function_expression,  effect, r_effect_closure).
handled(js, class_expression,           effect, r_effect_closure).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- LATENT. A function definition performs no operation: what its body does
-- happens at a CALL, and `eff_latent[flow]` is where it is carried. These four
-- kinds have no evaluation-site effect of their own, and saying that is a
-- positive statement about the construct rather than an absence.
--
-- `class_declaration` IS NOT HERE, and that is the measured half of this
-- group: a class declaration RUNS its static blocks and field initialisers at
-- declaration time, so it is not latent at all. It is open below.
handled(js, function_declaration,       effect, r_effect_latent).
handled(js, class_method,               effect, r_effect_latent).
handled(js, class_private_method,       effect, r_effect_latent).
handled(js, object_method,              effect, r_effect_latent).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- DEFINITION TIME AND CONSTRUCTION TIME — the five kinds that RUN at a moment
-- other than a call, 2026-09-09 (w_effect_class_initialisers). They are the
-- exact complement of the latent group above and that is why they could not
-- join it: a class declaration is not inert where it stands. It evaluates
-- `extends`, it calls its decorators, it evaluates every computed key, and it
-- runs every static block and every static field initialiser, in source order,
-- once. `r_effect_class_definition` is the join over those parts, seeded onto
-- the declaration NODE, which is the edge `eff_latent` could not supply because
-- there is no `resolves` row to carry it.
--
-- AND IT IS ALSO WHY THIS GROUP IS NOT ONE VERDICT. A STATIC field initialiser
-- and a static block run ONCE, when the class is defined; a NON-STATIC one runs
-- ONCE PER INSTANCE, at every `new`, and never at all if nothing constructs the
-- class. Two moments, and the model carries them as two relations —
-- `class_define_eff` and `class_construct_eff` — with
-- `eff_moment_both[audit]`/`eff_moment_unplaced[audit]` asserting that every
-- initialiser lands in exactly one. Collapsing them would have put an instance
-- initialiser's effect at a point in the program where it provably does not
-- happen, which is the one thing a may-set is not allowed to do.
--
-- THE CONSTRUCTION HALF HAS A CALL SITE AFTER ALL, which is the half of this
-- item's own note that turned out to be wrong: `may_be_node[flow]` says which
-- class a `new` expression constructs, so the instance row hangs on the `new`
-- exactly as a callee's row hangs on a call. Only the definition half needed an
-- edge of a new shape.
--
-- `class_accessor_property` IS MODELLED AS A FIELD AND ITS SYNTHESISED PAIR
-- ADDS NOTHING HERE. Its parser plugin is on, the corpus holds two nodes, and
-- both are non-static — so the site this verdict rests on is the construction
-- half. The get/set pair the language writes reads and writes a backing slot on
-- the same receiver that `o.a` is already a `read<h>` and `o.a = v` already a
-- `write<h>` of, so it contributes no label the member machinery does not
-- derive. That is the opposite of the control-flow layer's verdict for the same
-- kind, where the synthesised call IS the content.
handled(js, class_declaration,          effect, r_effect_class_definition).
handled(js, static_block,               effect, r_effect_class_definition).
handled(js, class_property,             effect, r_effect_class_init).
handled(js, class_private_property,     effect, r_effect_class_init).
handled(js, class_accessor_property,    effect, r_effect_class_init).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- STATE, WITH ITS HEAP. `w_alias_store` left `r_member_write` — a member
-- written is a member read — and this layer reads that machinery rather than
-- inventing one: `plain_assign[flow]` says which assignments are plain, and
-- `member_node_v[flow]` with `selects[flow]` says which nodes select a member.
-- What is added here is the DIRECTION and the HEAP.
--
--   `o.k = v`   write<h>            `o.k`      read<h>
--   `o.k += v`  read<h> and write<h>   — a compound assignment is both, and
--                                       `plain_assign` is exactly the relation
--                                       that tells them apart
--   `x = v`     write<local>        `x++`      read<local> and write<local>
--
-- READING AN IMMUTABLE BINDING IS NOT A `read<h>` AND THAT IS KOKA'S ANSWER,
-- not a shortcut taken to make a number look better: `read<h>` is a read of a
-- mutable CELL, and a `const` that nothing reassigns is a value. So the rule
-- seeds `read<local>` for a name something assigns to and for nothing else.
-- `identifier` is nevertheless an OPEN cell below, because a free name is a
-- third case this model cannot decide.
handled(js, assignment_expression,      effect, r_effect_state).
handled(js, update_expression,          effect, r_effect_state).
handled(js, member_expression,          effect, r_effect_state).
handled(js, optional_member_expression, effect, r_effect_state).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- TRANSFER. Seven kinds whose evaluation calls something, where the callee is
-- a node IN THIS PROGRAM and `resolves[code]` already names it — so the effect
-- propagation picks them up with no arm of its own, which is the whole reason
-- this layer could be built on top of the call graph rather than beside it.
--
-- FOUR OF THESE ARE HIDDEN CALLS and the control-flow layer had already found
-- them: `w_destructuring_hides_a_call` measured at run time that an array
-- pattern runs the iterator, an object spread runs the getters, and an object
-- rest runs the source's getters. `pattern_iterates` and `pattern_accessor`
-- turn each into a `resolves` row, so at this layer they are ordinary calls.
-- The residue — an iterator that belongs to the standard library rather than
-- to this program — is `call_expression`'s open cell and not four copies of it.
handled(js, decorator,                  effect, r_effect_transfer).
handled(js, super,                      effect, r_effect_transfer).
handled(js, tagged_template_expression, effect, r_effect_transfer).
handled(js, spread_element,             effect, r_effect_transfer).
handled(js, rest_element,               effect, r_effect_transfer).
handled(js, object_pattern,             effect, r_effect_transfer).
handled(js, array_pattern,              effect, r_effect_transfer).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- MODULE EVALUATION. Four kinds whose evaluation runs ANOTHER FILE'S TOP LEVEL,
-- so their effect is that module's top-level effect. This is a MISSING EDGE and
-- not a missing surface, which is what separates this group from the ambient
-- calls still open below: the module is in the corpus, `module_source[code]`
-- and `import_target[code]` in rules/js-dataflow.rofl already name it, and
-- crossing the boundary for a top-level EFFECT is the same join
-- `w_cg_module_boundary` already made for a NAME.
--
-- FOUR KINDS AND NOT TWO. `export * from` and the `export ... from` form of
-- `export_named_declaration` evaluate the source module exactly as an import
-- does — the re-export edge `w_mod_beyond_the_import` closed at the modules
-- layer, arriving here as an effect. `export_named_declaration` covers BOTH its
-- forms with one mechanism: the form with a `source` carries the target's row,
-- and the form without one performs nothing, which is the same positive
-- statement `export_default_declaration` is waived under.
--
-- AND THE DYNAMIC FORM IS IN THIS GROUP RATHER THAN IN A SUSPENSION ONE.
-- `import('./m')` evaluates m and returns a promise; the effect is m's row and
-- the suspension is CONTROL, exactly as `await_expression` is waived under
-- `a_suspension_is_not_an_effect`. There is no `load` label and no ordering
-- label anywhere in this layer, and `eff_import_invented[audit]` is the row
-- that says a site contributes what its target has and nothing else — the same
-- refusal `concrete_async_smuggled[audit]` makes for the concrete column.
--
-- THE FRONTIER IS A MISSING FILE, NOT A MISSING TABLE, and that is why these
-- four are answered while `call_expression` is not. A specifier naming a module
-- outside the corpus has no module to ask — `eff_import_outside[flow]` names
-- the three `./trace.mjs` sites — and adding the file makes the answer arrive
-- with no rule change. Nothing anybody can add to a corpus supplies
-- `ambient_effect`.
handled(js, import_declaration,         effect, r_effect_module).
handled(js, import_expression,          effect, r_effect_module).
handled(js, export_all_declaration,     effect, r_effect_module).
handled(js, export_named_declaration,   effect, r_effect_module).

-- ===========================================================================
-- WHAT THE LAYER WAIVES — 53 CELLS, AND NOT ONE OF THEM IS `total` BY DEFAULT
--
-- A waiver here says the CONSTRUCT PERFORMS NO OPERATION, which in this
-- taxonomy is the positive statement that its own contribution is the bottom of
-- the lattice. That is a claim that can be wrong, and three of the traps it
-- walks past are recorded beside the rows that avoided them.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ELEVEN THAT NEVER RUN. A TypeScript type position is erased before anything
-- executes, so it has no effect in the same sense that a comment has none —
-- and this is the one waiver group in the file whose reason is about the
-- COMPILER rather than about the language's semantics.
ignored(js, ts_string_keyword,               effect, a_erased_before_execution).
ignored(js, tsnull_keyword,                  effect, a_erased_before_execution).
ignored(js, tsnumber_keyword,                effect, a_erased_before_execution).
ignored(js, tsstring_keyword,                effect, a_erased_before_execution).
ignored(js, tstype_alias_declaration,        effect, a_erased_before_execution).
ignored(js, tstype_annotation,               effect, a_erased_before_execution).
ignored(js, tstype_parameter_instantiation,  effect, a_erased_before_execution).
ignored(js, tstype_query,                    effect, a_erased_before_execution).
ignored(js, tstype_reference,                effect, a_erased_before_execution).
ignored(js, tsunion_type,                    effect, a_erased_before_execution).
ignored(js, tsfunction_type,                 effect, a_erased_before_execution).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SIX LITERALS. A literal evaluates to a value with no identity and performs no
-- operation, so its row is the bottom. `reg_exp_literal` is DELIBERATELY NOT
-- HERE — it is `handled` above — and that one exclusion is what makes this
-- group a judgement rather than a sweep over everything whose name ends in
-- `_literal`. `template_literal` is not here either, for the opposite reason:
-- it is OPEN, because `${x}` coerces.
ignored(js, string_literal,             effect, a_a_literal_is_its_own_value).
ignored(js, numeric_literal,            effect, a_a_literal_is_its_own_value).
ignored(js, boolean_literal,            effect, a_a_literal_is_its_own_value).
ignored(js, null_literal,               effect, a_a_literal_is_its_own_value).
ignored(js, big_int_literal,            effect, a_a_literal_is_its_own_value).
ignored(js, template_element,           effect, a_a_literal_is_its_own_value).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THREE IMMUTABLE BINDINGS. `this`, `import.meta`, `new.target` and a private
-- name are each fixed for the duration of an activation, so reading one is not
-- a `read<h>`: there is no cell to read. `import.meta` is HOST-PROVIDED and
-- that is not the same thing as host I/O — obtaining it performs no operation,
-- and what a program does WITH it is the member's cell.
ignored(js, this_expression,            effect, a_an_immutable_binding_is_its_own_value).
ignored(js, meta_property,              effect, a_an_immutable_binding_is_its_own_value).
ignored(js, private_name,               effect, a_an_immutable_binding_is_its_own_value).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- TWENTY-FIVE CONTAINERS. A container performs no operation; it SEQUENCES or
-- SELECTS operations its parts perform, and those parts reach the enclosing
-- function through `nearest_v[flow]` — which is where the JOIN happens in this
-- layer. So the waiver is not "nothing happens here", it is "the join is not
-- this node's contribution", and the join is modelled.
--
-- THE SHORT-CIRCUIT PAIR IS IN THIS GROUP ON PURPOSE. `a && b` and `c ? d : e`
-- evaluate one arm sometimes and the other sometimes; the effect of the whole
-- is the JOIN of both arms, because this layer is a may-set. The control-flow
-- layer calls them `r_guard_arm` and this one calls them containers, and both
-- are true of the same node — the layers are asking different questions.
ignored(js, block_statement,            effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, expression_statement,       effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, sequence_expression,        effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, parenthesized_expression,   effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, if_statement,               effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, conditional_expression,     effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, logical_expression,         effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, return_statement,           effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, switch_statement,           effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, switch_case,                effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, labeled_statement,          effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, empty_statement,            effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, class_body,                 effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
-- A key/value pair in an object literal, INCLUDING the computed-key form:
-- `{ [k()]: v }` runs `k` and `{ a: f() }` runs `f`, and in both the operation
-- belongs to the child rather than to the pair. `object_method` is NOT here —
-- it is a function definition and is filed with the latent group.
ignored(js, object_property,            effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
-- A DECLARATION IS A CONTAINER AND ITS INITIALISER IS NOT. `const x = f()` runs
-- `f` and the declarator performs nothing of its own. A `let` does create a
-- mutable cell, which is an allocation in Koka's local heap — and it is not
-- counted here, because the cell's whole observable life is the
-- `read<local>` / `write<local>` pair this layer already derives, and counting
-- its creation as well would double-count one binding.
ignored(js, variable_declaration,       effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, variable_declarator,        effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
-- The two transparent TypeScript wrappers. `x!` and `x as T` are erased and
-- their OPERAND remains, so they are containers rather than members of the
-- erased group above — the distinction the value layer already makes when it
-- lists them as shapes and reads through them.
ignored(js, tsas_expression,            effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, tsnon_null_expression,      effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
-- A defaulted parameter is a container whose right arm runs conditionally,
-- which is exactly `guard_kind(assignment_pattern, right)` at the control-flow
-- layer seen from here.
ignored(js, assignment_pattern,         effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
-- The specifiers. A specifier NAMES what an import or export moves; the module
-- evaluation that the declaration triggers is the DECLARATION's cell, and those
-- are `handled` above under `r_effect_module`. The split is load-bearing rather
-- than tidy: a specifier's ERASURE marker is what decides whether the
-- declaration evaluates anything at all, and section 5c of the rules reads it
-- off the specifier while the effect stays on the declaration.
ignored(js, import_specifier,           effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, import_default_specifier,   effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, import_namespace_specifier, effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, export_specifier,           effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, export_namespace_specifier, effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, export_default_declaration, effect, a_a_container_performs_no_operation).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- TWO PURE TRANSFERS. `break` and `continue` move control and perform no
-- operation on any heap, throw nothing and allocate nothing. The control-flow
-- layer has a great deal to say about both and this layer has nothing, which is
-- the clearest case in the file of two layers disagreeing about how interesting
-- one kind is.
ignored(js, break_statement,            effect, a_a_transfer_performs_no_operation).
ignored(js, continue_statement,         effect, a_a_transfer_performs_no_operation).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ONE KIND THE PARSER NEVER EMITS. `import` is babel's `Import` node — the
-- CALLEE of a dynamic import in the grammar babel used to produce — and
-- `plugins: ['typescript']` never emits it: the dynamic form arrives as an
-- `import_expression` with a `source` child and no callee at all. The argument
-- is identical at the call-graph, dataflow and modules layers, where it is
-- already written out three times under this same atom; this is the fourth, and
-- it is written out rather than left to a default for the reason those three
-- give — a kind waived by silence is a kind nobody can find again.
ignored(js, import,                     effect, a_babel_emits_no_import_callee).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- TWO SUSPENSIONS, AND THIS IS THE TAXONOMY'S DECISION RATHER THAN THIS FILE'S.
-- `f_the_effect_taxonomy_already_exists_and_it_is_a_lattice` refused ASYNC as
-- an effect: Koka has no such effect — async is `io` plus first-class control —
-- and this repository measured the point independently, because `await` is
-- transparent to value flow and `yield` is not, so ASYNC-as-an-effect would
-- assert a uniformity our own measurement contradicts.
--
-- AND `await` CALLS NOTHING, which was measured rather than assumed:
-- `f_an_await_calls_no_library_method_and_two_notes_said_it_did` patched
-- `Promise.prototype.then` and counted ZERO calls for `await <native promise>`,
-- `await 5` and `await <user thenable>` alike, on two V8 versions, with
-- `p.then(f)` at 1 as the control. The specification's `Await` uses an INTERNAL
-- operation, so an await is not an observable call at all. The effect of
-- `await e` is therefore exactly the effect of `e`, and the suspension is
-- control rather than effect.
ignored(js, await_expression,           effect, a_suspension_is_not_an_effect).
ignored(js, yield_expression,           effect, a_suspension_is_not_an_effect).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THREE DIRECTIVES. `'use strict'` and a hashbang are not evaluated
-- constructs — and facts/js-statements.rofl already measured the one thing that
-- could have made them one: strict mode's two reachable consequences are
-- implicit throws and the `this` of a plain call, `may_throw` is seeded by
-- `throw_statement` and nothing else, and every file this scanner sees is
-- parsed as a module and therefore strict already.
ignored(js, directive,                  effect, a_not_an_evaluated_construct).
ignored(js, directive_literal,          effect, a_not_an_evaluated_construct).
ignored(js, interpreter_directive,      effect, a_not_an_evaluated_construct).

-- ===========================================================================
-- ONE CELL THAT IS NOBODY'S WORK — `debugger` is IRREDUCIBLE, not open.
--
-- With a debugger attached the statement suspends the program and hands control
-- to a host that can do anything, which is `io`; with none attached it does
-- nothing at all, which is `total`. Both readings are correct and WHICH ONE
-- HOLDS IS DECIDED AT RUN TIME, so this is `runtime_dependent` — the same
-- irreducible reason `import(pathVar)` carries — and not `not_yet`. It
-- therefore costs the queue nothing: `open_cell[audit]` requires
-- `our_unknown`, and no amount of work moves this one.
unknown_because(js, debugger_statement, effect, runtime_dependent).

-- ===========================================================================
-- WHAT IS OPEN — 13 CELLS, EVERY ONE OWNED BY NAME IN facts/worklist.rofl
--
-- EIGHTEEN BECAME THIRTEEN ON 2026-09-09: `w_effect_class_initialisers` closed
-- its five, and the group it used to occupy is now a `handled` block above.
--
-- The rows below are `not_yet` by DEFAULT and carry no `unknown_because`,
-- because `not_yet` is exactly what they are and writing it out would add a
-- word without adding a fact. What they DO carry is an owner, one
-- `claim(queued, ...)` each, in facts/worklist.rofl beside the item that owns
-- it. `sweeper` for this layer is 0 and `unqueued[audit]` is 0, which is the
-- pair of numbers that says every one of the thirteen has a name against it.
--
--   FOUR — THE AMBIENT SURFACE          w_effect_ambient_call
--     call_expression, optional_call_expression, new_expression, identifier
--     261 of the corpus's call sites do not resolve to a node in this program.
--     A call whose callee is outside the program has an effect this layer
--     cannot state, and `console.log` is `io` only if something says `console`
--     is a runtime surface. `identifier` is here for the same reason one level
--     down: a bare name is a local (bottom or `read<local>`), an import
--     (bottom), or a free global (`read<global>`, and a ReferenceError if it is
--     not there), and only the third needs the surface.
--
--   THREE — IMPLICIT COERCION           w_effect_implicit_coercion
--     binary_expression, unary_expression, template_literal
--     A RULE FIRES ON ALL THREE AND THE CELLS STAY OPEN, which is
--     `f_a_cell_can_be_open_while_a_rule_fires_on_its_kind` a second time and
--     is a measurement rather than a hedge. Section 5b of rules/js-effects.rofl
--     decides a conversion by its OPERAND: a primitive has no conversion method
--     to run, an in-program object that overrides neither `valueOf` nor
--     `toString` converts through the language's own prototype, and an object
--     that overrides one is an ordinary call. MEASURED over 369 coerced
--     operands: 223 primitive, 5 in-program objects — none of which overrides
--     either key — and 141 the value layer did not trace.
--
--     THOSE 141 ARE THE CELL. An operand the value layer lost MAY be an object
--     with an override, and the honest answer is the one
--     `eff_call_unattributed[flow]` already gives: a positive relation naming
--     the frontier. Seeding the TOP of the lattice instead was measured and
--     refused — it takes `eff_label_unseen` from two labels to none and makes
--     `effect_of` `top` for most of the corpus. Narrowing it is interprocedural
--     value flow, which is limit 3 of section 6 of the rules and a different
--     item; the rest of the residue is an operand that came from outside the
--     program, which is `w_effect_ambient_call`'s in-tray.
--
--   ONE — `with`                        w_inert_statements
--     with_statement
--     `with (o) { x }` turns every bare name in its body into a member read on
--     `o`, so it changes what a READ MEANS — which is this layer's `read<h>`
--     seed. It joins the four `with_statement` cells the owner already holds at
--     the other four layers, under the same item, and it waits on the same
--     decision about name resolution.
